# Pi workflow reference

Env-specific facts at their current state, dated; if behavior seems changed,
verify against the source repository and update this file. Sandbox, mode and
approval questions: `~/.pi/agent/docs/sandbox.md`; sweep measurements and audit
baselines: the chezmoi repo's `docs/agents-audit-log.md`.

## Launch and ownership (last verified 2026-09-26)

- Launch through the managed `pi` zsh function: it runs the repository's
  lockfile-pinned CLI (`node_modules/.bin/pi`) — no Brew install — disables
  native tools and ambient extensions, then loads the managed workflow and
  Herdr when present. A binary run outside it bypasses that.
- Pi loads the applied `~/.pi/agent/workflow`, not the repository source; its
  `node_modules` resolves through a symlink to the repository's pinned tree (pi's
  parent-directory lookup). A source edit reaches a session only via `chezmoi apply`.
- The repository's npm manifest/lockfile pins the CLI, extension dependencies
  and worker SDK as one unit (currently {{ index (fromJson (include "package.json")).dependencies "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent" }}), so they cannot skew. Pi
  breaks extension APIs across 0.x point releases: upgrade by bumping that pin,
  then `npm ci`, `npm run test:pi` and `chezmoi apply` (lifecycle scripts off).
- Settings merge by managed key: pi's `lastChangelogVersion` survives apply; a
  saved theme or `/thinking` level resets. Trust lives in `trust.json`.
  Authentication and session files stay unmanaged — sign in through Pi's normal
  OpenAI subscription login, and do not import another harness's auth files.

## Models (last verified 2026-09-25)

- Default: `{{ .agents.pi.defaults.provider }}/{{ index .subagent_tiers.pi .agents.pi.defaults.tier }}`, {{ .agents.pi.defaults.reasoning_effort }} reasoning.
- The approval classifier (MCP calls, remote-mutating shell verbs, unsandboxed requests)
  runs two tool-less stages: `{{ .agents.pi.defaults.classifier_filter.model }}` at {{ .agents.pi.defaults.classifier_filter.reasoning_effort }} reasoning screens each reviewed action; anything
  but allow goes to `{{ .agents.pi.defaults.classifier_judge.model }}` at {{ .agents.pi.defaults.classifier_judge.reasoning_effort }} reasoning on the same prompt, whose verdict is final.
  Model, text and cache key align; reasoning changes may miss cache.
- Specialist roles, from `subagents` in agents.yaml (which records each why):

| role | model | reasoning |
|---|---|---|
{{ range $name, $role := includeTemplate "pi-roles" (dict "root" .) | fromJson -}}
| {{ $name }} | `{{ $role.model }}` | {{ $role.thinking }} |
{{ end }}
- `workflow/children.mjs` rejects frontier children. The `{{ .subagent_tiers.pi.frontier }}` pin is in
  the SDK's subscription catalog; if dropped, fail unavailable, not an alias or API billing.
- All models draw one plan allowance at their own rate
  ([live rate card](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/pricing); figures in the audit log).
  API task cost is not Pro allowance; compare completed tasks including retries.
- Context window: 272,000 input tokens on the subscription route, from the pinned
  model catalog (2026-09-26); the model pages' 1,050,000 is API-key only. It has
  moved without notice: trust the live readout (status line, `/session`; pi has no
  `/status`). Raising `contextWindow` via `modelOverrides` only defers compaction
  until provider rejection. Revisit at the next SDK bump or if it reports ≠ 272K.

## Specialists and skills (last verified 2026-09-22)

- Specialists under `~/.pi/agent/agents` render from the shared roster, each with
  an explicit OpenAI model, tool list and mandatory child policy extension; native
  builtin names cannot substitute for missing `workspace_*` tools, and read-only
  specialists stay read-only when the root enters execution. Only `general-purpose`
  (every tool, top worker tier) may launch children, to Claude Code's same depth.
- Named `subagent` launches use the roster in the tool description and preflight.
  Children run in the background, up to twenty sharing the root policy (Claude
  Code's default); batch independent launches that each repay their handoff.
- Children start fresh and have no channel back: the pi-subagents intercom
  bridge is off in `extensions/subagent/config.json` (decision 2026-09-10).
- Schema and description expose managed keys/APIs only; executor and enforcement stay unchanged.
  Scripts/chains are refused. Status (fleet or transcript), stop, interrupt and non-recovering steer
  are enabled; project role overrides, revival and mutating management are not. Nesting children lack
  the native notifier and collect descendants with blocking `bg_wait` before synthesis (2026-09-22).
- Runs have a two-hour hard backstop and a best-effort checkpoint/stop steer five minutes before it.
  `bg_wait` window expiry is non-terminal; the separate headless `agent_end` auto-drain still has a
  30-minute limit. A nesting child collects results during its turn, not through that drain (2026-09-22).
- Transcript and status-line rendering follows the source repo's `docs/pi-design.md`;
  the undocumented surfaces it leans on are inventoried in `docs/pi-coupling.md`.
- Shared skills under `~/.agents/skills` load only by explicit `/skill:name`:
  pi lists skills in the system prompt only while a tool named `read` or `bash`
  is active, and the workflow exposes `workspace_*` (2026-09-26). Inside a
  child, pi-subagents' tool allowlist may leave the direct `mcp__*` tools
  inactive; the `mcp` proxy is always allowlisted and reaches the same servers.

## Web search (last verified 2026-09-23)

- `web_search` is the pinned `pi-web-search` plugin: OpenAI's server-side search
  on the ChatGPT-subscription Responses endpoint model calls use — no extra
  endpoint, credential or network path, and the provider's own terms grey zone.
- Each search is one extra model request on the usage windows, at the small tier
  set in `~/.pi/agent/web-search.json` from `agents.pi.search_tier` (2026-09-26).
- Roles with Claude's `WebSearch` get it; the plugin's Gemini-only `url_context`
  also registers and is in no role's list. Fetching stays with the Exa MCP tools
  and Playwright; search accepts extra `urls` to analyse alongside the query.

## MCP and code intelligence (last verified 2026-09-22)

- Context7 tools stay direct (e.g. `mcp__context7_query-docs`); Exa and Playwright
  stay behind the `mcp` proxy: `mcp({ search: "…" })` finds a tool in the cached
  catalog, `mcp({ describe: "<name>" })` shows its schema, `mcp({ tool: "<name>",
  args: { … } })` calls it; a server shown as not connected connects on first call.
- MCP servers other than Playwright run inside SRT; Node servers use
  `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` so fetch follows the sandbox proxy. If every call fails
  with `fetch failed`, check that setting, then the allowed domains. The browser
  exception and its permissions are documented in `sandbox.md`.
- Configuration is explicit; other harnesses' private config is not imported. MCP
  scripting, namespace proxies, semantic search (`jev: false`), sampling and
  automatic OAuth discovery are disabled; the approval broker checks resolved
  server/tool/arguments, not just the outer proxy call.
- `freezeDirectTools: true` freezes registration after initialization, not cache updates: new tools
  remain reachable through `mcp`. Initial refresh can occur on first lazy connect; live owners are
  session-local, shared metadata caching is not fully isolated (2026-09-22).
- No LSP: diagnostics come from project toolchains via `workspace_bash` (`tsc --noEmit`,
  `go vet`, pyright, linters); rationale in `docs/pi-implementation.md` Decisions.
- Claude's proprietary plugins, cloud services and exact UI interactions are
  not supplied by these packages; keep the other harness for those.
